¶
{{smaller block|He might, methinks, have stood one ''brush'' with them, and have yielded when there had been no remedy.
{{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim’s Progress, p. 188.}}}}
'''11.''' An application of a brush, as in sweeping or dusting; a brushing; a removal as if with a brush: as, give my hat a ''brush.'' [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|Leaves . . . have with one winter’s ''brush''<br>Fell from their boughs.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., iv. 3.}}}}
'''12.''' A painter; one who uses a brush: as, a brother ''brush.''—{{smaller|'''Haidinger’s brushes,''' optical figures, early described by the Austrian mineralogist W. von Haidinger (1795-1871), appearing like colored brushes, sometimes resembling the ordinary interference-figures (see ''interference'') of a biaxial crystal, observed with ordinary transmitted light in sections of certain minerals, especially those which effect a marked absorption of color, as andalusite, iolite, etc. The term also includes the peculiar phenomenon of four small colored tufts observed by some persons with the naked eye, by others when a Nicol prism is used, upon looking at a bright light, as a white cloud. The latter phenomenon is supposed to be due to the polarizing action of the eye itself.—'''Hydraulic brush.''' See ''hydraulic.''—'''Revolving brush,''' a cylindrical brush supported in a frame and made to revolve rapidly on an axis by gearing or other mechanism. Such brushes are used for street-sweeping, and also by barbers.—'''Rotary brush.''' Same as ''revolving brush.''='''Syn. 10.''' ''Rencounter, Skirmish,'' etc. See ''encounter.''}}

'''brush''' (brush), ''v.'' [< ME. ''bruschen,'' < OF. ''brosser,'' v. i., beat the brush or thicket for game, scour the country, also simply cross, pass, F. ''brosser'' (= Sp. ''brozar,'' brush), < ''brosse,'' brush, thicket: see ''brush, n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To sweep or rub with a brush: as, to ''brush'' a hat.
{{smaller block|The robes to kepe well & also to ''brusche'' them clenly.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (ed. Furnivall), p. 180.}}
Let their heads be sleekly combed, their blue coats ''brushed.''
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., iv. 1.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Dark wiry hair ''brushed'' on one side.
{{right|''Bulwer'', Pelham, xl.}}}}
'''2.''' To remove by brushing or by lightly passing over: as, to ''brush'' off dust.
{{smaller block|<poem>Though from off the boughs each morn
We ''brush'' mellifluous dews.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., v. 429.}}}}
{{smaller block|I think the very best thing is to ''brush'' all the old Dons off the stage.
{{right|''Disraeli'', Coningsby, v. 2.}}}}
'''3.''' To sweep or touch as with a brush; strike lightly by passing over the surface; pass lightly over: as, to ''brush'' the arm in passing.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Brush’d'' with the hiss of rustling wings.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 768.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A thousand nights have ''brush’d'' their balmy wings
Over these eyes.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
'''4.''' Figuratively, to ruffle; excite.
{{smaller block|Poor Silas’s loss served to ''brush'' the slow current of Raveloe conversation.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Silas Marner, x.}}}}
'''5.''' To furnish with brushes or branches of dead trees to climb on: as, to ''brush'' peas.—{{smaller|'''To brush up''', to furbish; polish; renovate; hence, to improve in any way; make brighter or clearer, as the memory or past knowledge.}}
{{smaller block|You have commissioned me to paint your shop, and I have done my best to ''brush'' you ''up'' like your neighbours.
{{right|''Pope.''}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To move quickly or in haste; rush: as, to ''brush'' past a person.
{{smaller block|Then Pollux . . . ''brusshit'' into batell.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'', l. 1216.}}}}
{{smaller block|Snatching his hat, he ''brushed'' off like the wind.
{{right|''Goldsmith.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Brush’d''
Thro’ the dim meadow toward his treasure-trove.</poem>}}

